  {
    "index": 3,
    "hash": "oFiw3pTJFXxolMFOhGebn3uuC7Y=",
    "timestampUtcMs": "2026-08-14T12:34:47.000Z",
    "action": {
      "type": "SET_CONTENT",
      "input": {
        "content": "`document-drive` keeps its child catalogue **inside the drive document's own state**, at `state.global.nodes[]`. Every `ADD_FILE` mutates that array, which means three costs grow together with child count: every replay walks every previously-added child, every read materialises the full node list, and the drive's operation log grows one entry per file. Container performance decays as the drive fills.\n\n`ADD_RELATIONSHIP` sidesteps all three. It is a **system-scope action handled directly by the reactor's job executor** \u2014 the source document's state and operation log are untouched. The indexer writes one row to the `DocumentRelationship` table, and the graph is read back through `IDocumentIndexer`. Cost is flat in the number of children.\n\nThe `drive-override` recipe demonstrates this by building a container document whose entire schema is:\n\n```graphql\ntype CustomContainerState {\n  name: String!\n  description: String\n}\n```\n\nNo `nodes`, no children, no files. It then links 10,000 children via `reactor.execute(containerId, \"main\", [addRelationshipAction(containerId, childId, \"contains\")])` and confirms `state.global` keys are still exactly `[\"description\", \"name\"]`.\n\nEnumeration is paged and DB-native rather than a state read:\n\n- `documentIndexer.getOutgoing(containerId, [\"contains\"], { cursor, limit })` \u2014 children; advance `cursor` until `nextCursor` is empty.\n- `documentIndexer.getIncoming(childId)` \u2014 parents. The relationship table is indexed on both `sourceId` and `targetId`, so the graph is genuinely bidirectional.\n\nThe same data is exposed over GraphQL as `documentOutgoingRelationships(documentId, relationshipTypes, paging)` and `documentIncomingRelationships(...)`, both paged, for external clients.\n\nThe trade-off: the container's reducer never sees relationships at all, so any invariant you wanted to enforce over the child set (\"no more than N children\", \"child types must match\") is no longer enforceable in the reducer. You gain flat scaling and lose reducer-level control over membership.\n\nProvenance: academy `05-Lookup/00-Cookbook.md`, Drive Override recipe (\"Why this scales better than ADD_FILE\"), academy 6.2.2-dev.45.",
        "updatedAt": "2026-08-14T12:34:47.000Z"
      },
      "timestampUtcMs": "2026-08-14T12:34:47.000Z"
    }
  },
